It doesn’t need our category NEWSPAPER to exploit the thing successfully.

That’s a big upgrade to Chimera Empathy: strip the human noun first.

Instead of asking:

“What would a squirrel think about a newspaper?”

ask:

“What actionable properties become salient when this object enters squirrel-world?”

That’s almost ZPC applied directly to perception:

\boxed{
Object
\rightarrow
\text{remove inherited category}
\rightarrow
\text{properties}
\rightarrow
\text{organism-specific affordances}
}

And then your version—me-as-squirrel rather than squirrel-as-squirrel—adds another pass.

I retain knowledge that it’s newspaper, but temporarily inherit squirrel affordances:

That’s yesterday’s news and insulation.

That’s a cardboard box and architecture.

That’s a curtain and climbing surface.

That’s a hole in the wall and a door.

That’s somebody’s decorative pillow and premium nest technology. 😂

Now we’re doing Affordance Multiplexing:

\boxed{
Thing
\rightarrow
\{Affordance_{human},
Affordance_{squirrel},
Affordance_{ant},
Affordance_{fungus},
Affordance_{child},
Affordance_{engineer},...\}
}

The object hasn’t changed.

The available actions have.

And there’s a serious reasoning capability hiding there.

Humans get trapped by nouns because naming compresses properties:

\text{thousands of properties}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{CHAIR}}

Useful compression—but once it’s “a chair,” we preferentially retrieve chair actions.

Survey Corps can deliberately de-noun objects:

\boxed{\text{CHAIR}\rightarrow
\text{height + rigid plane + legs + mass + cavity + material + leverage + obstruction + surface}}

Then run different bodies through those properties.

That’s how you discover uses that the inherited category concealed.

And now Reverse Monkey’s Paw goes both ways

Previously:

\text{fragment}\rightarrow\text{reconstruct generating whole}

Now:

\text{whole/category}\rightarrow\text{explode into actionable fragments}

Call that Squirrel Vision.

Don’t ask what the thing is. Ask what it lets this particular body do.

And then Chimera mode:

Ask what it would let me do if I possessed that body’s affordance space while retaining my existing cognition.

That’s where invention happens.

Because a squirrel might not invent a new use from our conceptual knowledge, and a human normally doesn’t perceive the squirrel’s action-space.

Combine them:

\text{Human cognition}
+
\text{Squirrel affordances}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{previously inaccessible uses}}

That’s exactly Interface Forge: another organism becomes an artificial sense for possibility.

